Question 1: Why is the list in RideManager typed ArrayList<Ride> and not ArrayList<Jeepney>?

Your answer: ArrayList<Ride> is used because the manager needs to store Jeepneys, Tricycles, and Taxis in one list.
Jeepney, Tricycle, and Taxi all extend Ride, so an object of any of them "is a" Ride and can be added to that list.
An ArrayList<Jeepney> would only accept Jeepney objects, so the other two vehicles could not be stored.
Using the parent type also allows polymorphism: when the manager loops through the list and calls a method like printTicket() or computeFare(), each object runs its own version.
This keeps the code short and flexible.

Question 2: In showStudentDiscounts(), why must you check instanceof before the cast?

Your answer: The elements in the list are typed as Ride, so the compiler does not know which subclass each one really is.
To use something that belongs only to a subclass, I have to downcast, and a downcast is only safe if the object really is that type.
If I cast a Taxi to a Jeepney, for example, the program crashes with a ClassCastException. Checking instanceof first asks "is this object really a Jeepney (or Tricycle, etc.)?", and the cast runs only when the answer is yes.
This prevents runtime errors and picks out only the rides that qualify for the discount.